Ok, I dunno what it is, but whenever I start playing nethack I always wind up
getting distracted into thinking about how it would work multiplayer. (And the
last several times this has happened, I've eventually gotten discouraged or
disguested with the idea and given up. Yeehah.) Anyway, there seem to be a lot
of limitations in the game that would need changing to make it viable for
multiplayer.

First, the dungeon is really too small for any large party of characters to
be rampaging thru. This is probably the biggest hassle right there, what do
you do with 5 guys (and their pets!) starting at the staircase on level 1... 

Next, you need a decent time-base to keep the game moving, but without it
going by too fast. (Maybe 1/5th real time? Give each player up to 5 seconds to
execute a move, which amounts to 1 second of game time?)

Also, you need to separate the program into a client and server, where the
server sends coded commands for the client to interpret as terminal/screen
control, etc. (lets you then create termcap or X based clients...)

I've seen a number of other queries along this topic answered with "go play
mud" which I suppose captures the multiplayer gaming aspect really well, but
still isn't quite the same flavor as what a multiplayer hack might be. You
would certainly want to add a lot of the parsing that mud provides, to allow
for more kinds of interaction with other players and critters, but it would
be nice if the game were still screen-based overall instead of line-mode...

(Say, while I'm at it, whatever happened to Quest, you know, the variant game
you could compile from the Hack 1.03 source that used natural-looking caverns
and tunnels instead of rectangular rooms?)

Hm. Maybe the entire hack dungeon needs some rethinking... The game seems to
have added a number of features from AD&D and such, but the dungeon itself has
no real rhyme or reason to it. Then again, maybe that's the whole reason to
play the game, because it's just a hack and slash adventure with no real
thinking required.

(And I suppose it would get pretty tedious having all the interesting stuff
like altars and shops only on the surface, and having to run back there all
the time.)

So - this topic keeps coming up often enough, but nothing seems to ever come
of it. Is it really so hard, would it change the game so much, is it really
just not worth the effort?
